Boxing & Unboxing 
Jab aap kisi Value Type (jaise int, char, bool) ko kisi Reference Type (jaise object ya koi Interface) ke andar daalte hain, toh usko Boxing kehte hain.
int num = 100;        // Yeh ek Value Type hai (Stack par hai)
object obj = num;     // 🎁 BOXING ho gayi! (Stack ki value Heap par object me pack ho gayi)

Unboxing kya hoti hai? (Reference Type $\rightarrow$ Value Type)
Jab aap us boxed object se wapas apni original Value Type ki value nikalte hain, toh usko Unboxing kehte hain.
object obj = 100;             // Pehle se boxed object hai
int unboxedNum = (int)obj;    // 📦 UNBOXING ho gayi! (Heap se nikal kar wapas int me cast kiya)

const (Compile-Time Constant)
Kab evaluate hota hai? Code compile hone se pehle (Compile-time).
Kiske sath lag sakta hai? Sirf primitive types (int, double, bool, string) aur null ke sath. Kisi custom class ya object ke sath const nahi lag sakta.

readonly (Run-Time Constant)
Kab evaluate hota hai? Jab program run hota hai aur object banta hai (Runtime).
Kiske sath lag sakta hai? Kisi bhi datatype ke sath—chahe wo int ho, string ho, ya koi bada class object ho!

Seedhi baat:
Agar value aisi hai jo zindagi bhar kabhi nahi badlegi (jaise PI = 3.14 ya DaysInAWeek = 7), toh const use karo.
Agar value aisi hai jo object banne par ya constructor ke through set honi hai (jaise API_Key ya ConnectionString), toh readonly use karo!

Managed Code (.NET ki Chhaon me)
Kaun chalata hai? Isko CLR (Common Language Runtime) execute karta hai.
Khel kya hai? Jab tu C# me code likhta hai, toh pehle wo Intermediate Language (IL) me compile hota hai, aur phir CLR usko machine code me badalta hai.

Fayde:

Garbage Collector (GC): Faltu memory ko saaf karne ki tension developer ki nahi, GC ki hoti hai.
Type Safety: C# compiler pehle hi check kar leta hai ki tu galat datatype me kuch ulta-seedha toh nahi daal raha.
Exception Handling: Runtime khud errors aur exceptions ko aaram se pakadta hai.
Examples: C#, F#, VB.NET me likha gaya poora code Managed Code hota hai.

2. Unmanaged Code (Azaad aur Khatarnak)
Kaun chalata hai? Yeh seedha OS (Windows/Linux) aur CPU par chalta hai. Isko .NET ya CLR se koi matlab nahi hota.
Khel kya hai? Isme sab kuch developer ke bharose hota hai.

Nuqsaan / Khatra:
Manual Memory Management: Memory allocate bhi khud karni padti hai (malloc se) aur delete bhi khud karni padti hai (free ya delete se). Agar bhool gaya, toh Memory Leak ho jayegi!
No Garbage Collector: GC yahan nahi hota.
Unsafe pointers: Yeh memory ke direct address ke sath khel sakta hai, isliye crash hone ke chances zyada hote hain.


Main Fark (Ek Line me)
Delegate: Ek Function Pointer (ya method ka reference) hota hai. Yeh ek variable ki tarah hota hai jo kisi method ko pakad kar rakh sakta hai.
Khatra: Isko koi bhi bahar wala banda invoke (chala) sakta hai ya overwrite kar sakta hai.

Event: Delegate ke upar ek Security Layer (Encapsulation) hoti hai. Isko sirf wahi class invoke kar sakti hai jisne isko banaya hai.
Bahar wale classes sirf subscribe (+=) ya unsubscribe (-=) kar sakte hain, poora control nahi le sakte.

// Yeh ek delegate ban gaya jo kisi bhi aisi method ko point kar sakta hai jo void leti ho
public delegate void ProcessHandler();

public class Process
{
    public ProcessHandler ProcessStarted; // Yeh ek delegate variable hai
}
2. Ab aate hain Event par (Security Guard)
Event, delegate ko secure karne ka tareeqa hai. Jab aap delegate ke aage event keyword laga dete ho, toh C# compiler usko lock kar deta hai.

Bahar wale classes sirf += (subscribe) aur -= (unsubscribe) kar sakte hain.

Bahar wale classes na toh = null karke list saaf kar sakte hain, aur na hi khud us event ko fire (invoke) kar sakte hain!

Chhota sa Real-World Example dekh:
Analogy: Socho ek Fire Alarm (Aag ka alarm) hai.

Delegate: Socho ek aam bell hai jiska connection kisi ke bhi haath me diya ja sakta hai—koi bhi aakar wire kaat de ya baja de.

Event: Fire alarm building ki diwar par laga hai. Tum sirf yeh keh sakte ho ki "Mujhe is alarm ki aawaz sunai deni chahiye" (+= subscription)
. Lekin alarm bajana kisne hai? Sirf sensor ya building authority (Publisher Class) hi usko trigger kar sakti hai, koi raste chalta aam aadmi jaakar zabardasti alarm nahi baja sakta!

Do Tarah ke Scenarios Hote Hain:
1. Jab dono ka kaam same ho (Implicit Resolution)
Agar Interface A aur Interface B dono me ek method hai Show(), aur class ke andar unka logic ek hi jaisa chalna hai, toh C# bohot samajhdar hai—tu class ke andar sirf ek hi method bana de, wo dono interfaces ki zaroorat ko poora kar dega!

2. Asli Conflict (Jab dono ka kaam alag-alag ho!) — Explicit Interface Implementation
Soch ek Machine class hai jo do interfaces implement kar rahi hai:

IPrinter (jiska Print() method paper print karta hai)

IScanner (jiska Print() method actually document scan karke print jaisa kuch karta hai, ya dono ke kaam bilkul alag hain).

Agar dono ka method signature void Print() hai, toh tu class ke andar ek common method nahi bana sakta. Iske liye C# me Explicit Implementation use hoti hai, jahan hum method ke aage interface ka naam jod dete hain!

Aao Code se Samjhein:
C#
using System;

// Pehla Interface
public interface IPrinter
{
    void Print();
}

// Doosra Interface (Same method name!)
public interface IScanner
{
    void Print();
}

// Class jo dono ko implement kar rahi hai
public class MultiFunctionMachine : IPrinter, IScanner
{
    // --- EXPLICIT IMPLEMENTATION ---
    // Note: Yahan 'public' ya 'private' keyword nahi lagate!
    
    void IPrinter.Print()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Printer interface wala Print() chal raha hai... 🖨️");
    }

    void IScanner.Print()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Scanner interface wala Print() chal raha hai... 📠");
    }
}

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        MultiFunctionMachine machine = new MultiFunctionMachine();

        // ❌ Error: machine.Print(); 
        // Seedha call nahi kar sakte kyunki compiler confuse hai ki kaunsa Print() chalaye!

        // ✅ Sahi Tareeqa (Interface ke reference se call karna padega):
        
        IPrinter printer = machine;
        printer.Print(); // Output: Printer interface wala Print() chal raha hai...

        IScanner scanner = machine;
        scanner.Print(); // Output: Scanner interface wala Print() chal raha hai...
    }
}
Interview ke liye 3 Golden Points (Yaad rakhne wale):
Access Modifiers nahi hote: Jab aap explicit implementation karte hain (void IPrinter.Print()), toh uske aage public, private, protected kuch bhi likhne ki permission nahi hoti. Yeh by default private ho jaate hain class ke andar se.

Direct Call nahi hota: Aap object.MethodName() karke seedha call nahi kar sakte. Usko chalane ke liye object ko pehle uske Interface type me cast karna padta hai (jaise upar IPrinter printer = machine; kiya).

Kyu zaroori hai? Yeh tab kaam aata hai jab aapko aisi third-party libraries ya multiple interfaces use karne padein jinke methods ke naam aapas me takra (conflict) rahe hon.

Seedhi baat:
Eager Loading (.Include()): Hum pehle hi bol dete hain — "Bhai, customer ke sath orders bhi le aa." (Ek hi baar me sab aa jata hai).

using (var context = new AppDbContext())
{
    // Eager Loading: Customer ke sath uske saare Orders ek hi query me aa gaye!
    var customer = context.Customers
                          .Include(c => c.Orders) 
                          .FirstOrDefault(c => c.Id == 1);

    // Yahan database se pehle hi orders aa chuke hain, koi nayi query nahi chalegi.
    foreach (var order in customer.Orders)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(order.OrderDate);
    }
}

Lazy Loading: Hum pehle sirf customer laate hain, aur jaise hi hum code me customer.Orders likhte hain, EF Core chupchap piche se ek aur query chala kar orders utha laata hai.

using (var context = new AppDbContext())
{
    // Yahan sirf Customer aaya, Orders nahi aaye!
    var customer = context.Customers.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Id == 1);

    // Abhi tak orders database se nahi aaye the...
    // LEKIN jaise hi humne neeche wali line likhi, EF Core background me chupchap ek nayi SQL query chala dega!
    foreach (var order in customer.Orders) 
    {
        Console.WriteLine(order.OrderDate);
    }
}
Virtual Property ka Khel:
Lazy loading tabhi kaam karti hai jab tune Entity class me navigation property ke aage virtual keyword lagaya ho (jaise: public virtual ICollection<Order> Orders { get; set; }).

EF Core ka Magic Proxy:
Jab tu context.Customers.FirstOrDefault() likhta hai, toh EF Core asli Customer class ka ek Duplicate (Proxy) Object memory me banakar deta hai. Is proxy object ko pata hota hai ki mere andar ek Orders property hai jo abhi khali (null) hai.

Interception (Jaise hi haath lagaya, query chal gayi!):

Jab tak tu customer.Orders ko haath nahi lagata, database se koi query nahi chalti.

ConfigureServices (Dependency Injection ka Adda)
Kab chalta hai? Jab application start hoti hai (ek hi baar).
Kaam kya hai? Iska kaam hai .NET ke built-in DI Container ko batana ki hamare poore project me kaun-kaun si services available rahengi.
Aap yahan repositories, database contexts, aur third-party services ko add (AddScoped, AddTransient, AddSingleton) karte hain.
public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
{
    // Yahan hum .NET container ko bata rahe hain ki hamare paas ye services hain:
    services.AddControllers();
    
    // Database Context register karna
    services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(options => 
        options.UseSqlServer(Configuration.GetConnectionString("DefaultConnection")));

    // Apni custom services add karna
    services.AddScoped<IUserRepository, UserRepository>();
}

Configure (HTTP Request Pipeline & Middleware)
Kab chalta hai? Application start hone par (ConfigureServices ke theek baad).
Kaam kya hai? Yeh method define karta hai ki jab bhi client (browser ya Angular app) se koi HTTP Request server par aayegi, toh wo kis sequence me process hogi.

public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
{
    // 1. Error handling pehle aayegi
    if (env.IsDevelopment())
    {
        app.UseDeveloperExceptionPage();
    }

    // 2. Routing
    app.UseRouting();

    // 3. Authentication (Pehle pehchan ho)
    app.UseAuthentication();

    // 4. Authorization (Fir permission check ho)
    app.UseAuthorization();

    // 5. Endpoints (Controllers tak request pahunche)
    app.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
    {
        endpoints.MapControllers();
    });
}
Sabse Badi Interview Trick (Middleware Order): Configure ke andar middlewares ka sequence (order) bohot mayne rakhta hai! 
Agar aapne Authentication ko Authorization ke baad laga diya, toh app crash ya fail ho jayegi. Pehle Authentication check hoga, tabhi toh Authorization decide hoga!



Main Definition (Ek Line me)
HTTP Request Pipeline un saare Middlewares ka ek sequence (katar) hai jiske through ek incoming HTTP Request gujarti hai, aur wahi request ya uska response ulte raste (reverse order) me wapas client ke paas jata hai.

Real-World Analogy: Airport Security Checkpoint ✈️
Socho tum airport par enter karte ho aur flight pakadne jaate ho:

Gate 1: Security guard ticket check karta hai (Authentication).
Gate 2: Baggage Scanner check karta hai ki koi illegal cheez toh nahi hai (Authorization / Validation).
Gate 3: Boarding pass stamp hota hai (Logging / Performance monitoring).
Final Gate (Plane): Tum apne actual seat (Controller / Endpoint) par baithte ho.

Agar tumhare paas ticket nahi hai, toh pehla gate hi tumhe rok dega, aage jaane hi nahi dega! Wahi haal Request Pipeline ka hota hai.

1. Middleware kya hota hai?
Pipeline ke andar jo chote-chote components lage hote hain, unko Middleware kehte hain. Har middleware ke paas do kaam hote hain:
Request ko aage wale middleware ke paas pass karna (await next()).
Ya fir request ko wahin rok kar direct response wapas bhej dena (jaise agar authentication fail ho gayi toh wahin se 401 Unauthorized return kar dena).

Middleware – Use(), Run(), Map() ✅

app.Use() – The Chain Builder (Chain ko aage badhane wala)
Yeh kya karta hai? Yeh sabse common method hai. Yeh middleware me logic execute karta hai, aur uske baad aglai middleware ko request pass karne ki taaqat (next()) rakhta hai.

Khel: Yeh "choti-choti daaliyan" jodne jaisa hai. Yeh request ko aage bhi bhej sakta hai aur agar chahe toh wahin rok bhi sakta hai (short-circuit).
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    Console.WriteLine("1. Request aayi, humne kuch check kiya...");
    
    // Aage wale middleware ko bulaya (agar yeh nahi likhoge, toh pipeline wahin ruk jayegi!)
    await next(); 
    
    Console.WriteLine("3. Response wapas jaate waqt fir se yahan aae!");
});


app.Run() – The Terminator (Khel Khatam!)
Yeh kya karta hai? Yeh ek Terminal Middleware hota hai. Iska matlab yeh hai ki iske baad pipeline me koi doosra middleware nahi chal sakta.

Khel: Isme next parameter hota hi nahi hai! Yeh request ko pakadta hai, apna response likhta hai, aur game over kar deta hai (client ko response bhej deta hai).

Kab use hota hai? Aam taur par error pages dikhane ke liye ya jab aapko pata ho ki ab bas yahin se final response dena hai.
// Iske baad agar koi aur middleware likhoge, toh wo kabhi chalega hi nahi!
app.Run(async context =>
{
    await context.Response.WriteAsync("Hello from Run Middleware! Pipeline khatam.");
});

. app.Map() – The Branching Master (Rasta Badalne wala)
Yeh kya karta hai? Yeh URL ke path ke base par pipeline ki branch (alag rasta) bana deta hai.

Khel: Agar aap chahte hain ki jab bhi koi /admin URL par aaye, toh ek alag set of middlewares chalein, aur baaki sabke liye normal pipeline chale, tab aap Map() use karte hain.
// Agar URL '/admin' se shuru hota hai, toh yeh alag branch me chala jayega
app.Map("/admin", adminApp =>
{
    adminApp.Run(async context =>
    {
        await context.Response.WriteAsync("Welcome to Admin Dashboard area!");
    });
});

// Yeh normal pipeline hai jo baaki saare URLs ke liye chalegi
app.Run(async context =>
{
    await context.Response.WriteAsync("Welcome to Home Page!");
});


