AsNoTracking() – Read-Only Queries ka Superhero
Problem: Jab aap EF Core se data nikaalte hain (context.Users.ToList()), toh EF Core chupchap har object ko apne Change Tracker me yaad rakh leta hai. Kyu? Taki agar aap baad me us data ko badlein (user.Name = "New"), toh SaveChanges() chalne par wo database me update kar sake.

Nuqsaan: Agar aapko sirf data read karna hai (jaise GET API jisme sirf list dikhani hai), toh Change Tracker ki koi zaroorat nahi hai. Phir bhi EF Core har object ko track karne me memory aur CPU khapata hai!

Solution (AsNoTracking): Yeh EF Core ko bolta hai: "Bhai, mujhe data sirf padhne ke liye chahiye, isko yaad mat rakh." Isse query 30% se 50% tak fast ho jati hai aur memory bach jati hai.

2. Caching (Database ka Chakkar Khatam!)
Problem: Agar 1000 log ek hi API call kar rahe hain (jaise "Country List" ya "App Configuration"), aur har baar database par query ja rahi hai, toh database baith jayega!

Solution: Data ko ek baar database se uthao aur Server ki RAM (Memory) me save kar lo. Agli baar jab request aayegi, toh database jaane ki jagah seedha RAM se uthakar de do.

ASP.NET Core me do tarah ki caching hoti hai:

In-Memory Cache (IMemoryCache): Sirf ek hi server ki RAM me data save hota hai (choti ya single-server apps ke liye).
